---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BreadcrumbSchema from '../../components/BreadcrumbSchema.astro';
import { amsecSecurityScanner as product } from '../../data/amsec-security-scanner';
import '../../styles/amsec-security-scanner.css';
---

<BaseLayout noindex title="AmSec Security Scanner for WordPress" description="AmSec Security Scanner is a free WordPress security plugin: hardening checks, a malware scan, file integrity, login protection and traffic controls. Every finding shows its evidence.">
  <BreadcrumbSchema items={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Plugins', url: '/plugins/' }, { name: product.name, url: product.path }]} />
  <section class="as-shell">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="as-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/plugins/">Plugins</a><span>/</span><span aria-current="page">AmSec Security Scanner</span></nav>
      <div class="as-hero">
        <div>
          <p class="as-kicker">{product.status} · Free WordPress plugin</p>
          <h1 class="display as-title">AmSec Security Scanner.</h1>
          <p class="as-lede">Security checks that show their evidence. AmSec Security Scanner scans your site and shows you what it found and where. Nothing changes until you approve it.</p>
          <div class="as-actions"><a href="#features" class="btn btn-primary">See what it does</a><a href="/contact/#general" class="btn btn-ghost">Ask a question</a></div>
          <p class="as-small">{product.availability} {product.requirements}</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="as-receipt">
          <div class="as-receipt-head"><span>Example finding</span><span>Malware scan</span></div>
          <span class="as-verdict">Suspected code</span>
          <h2>A PHP file in uploads looks like a web shell</h2>
          <table class="as-evidence"><tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">File</th><td><code>/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/cache.php</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Evidence</th><td>Line 3 runs code taken straight from the request. The finding names the rule that matched.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Next step</th><td>Check the file with your host. The plugin hasn't deleted or changed it.</td></tr>
          </tbody></table>
          <figcaption>Example layout, not a scan of your site.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="as-shell" id="features"><div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="as-heading">What's in version {product.version}</h2>
    <p class="as-intro">Every protection is off when you install the plugin. Nothing runs in the background until you press Scan for the first time. After that, the scan repeats once a day.</p>
    <div class="as-list">{product.capabilities.map(capability => <article><h3>{capability.label}</h3><p>{capability.description}</p></article>)}</div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="as-shell"><div class="wrap as-split">
    <div><h2 class="as-heading">Your files stay on your server.</h2></div>
    <div class="as-prose"><p>The scans run on your own server. File contents, passwords and personal data are never sent anywhere, and AmazingPlugins doesn't receive any data from your site.</p><p>The plugin contacts outside services only for the features that need them. WordPress.org provides checksums and release files for the integrity scan. WPVulnerability answers version lookups after you turn them on. Pwned Passwords gets the first five characters of a password hash after you turn on breached-password checks. The plugin's readme lists exactly what each one receives.</p></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="as-shell"><div class="wrap as-split">
    <div><h2 class="as-heading">AI can suggest. You approve.</h2></div>
    <div class="as-prose"><p>On WordPress 6.9 or newer, with the official MCP Adapter plugin, an administrator can let an AI assistant list findings and run the local hardening checks. It can also propose a sign-in limit change. You see the proposal in wp-admin, and nothing changes until you approve it.</p><p>The assistant can't approve its own proposal, start a file repair or run arbitrary commands. This is off by default.</p></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="as-shell"><div class="wrap as-split">
    <div><h2 class="as-heading">What it doesn't do.</h2></div>
    <div class="as-prose"><ul><li>It doesn't have a maintained malware signature feed. Its rules update with plugin releases.</li><li>It doesn't remove malware or rebuild a whole plugin.</li><li>It doesn't check from outside your server whether a file is publicly reachable.</li><li>It doesn't have a built-in authenticator app. It points you to the Two Factor plugin instead.</li><li>Daily scans run on WP-Cron, so a quiet site may run them late.</li></ul><p>If you need a managed firewall rule feed today, compare it with <a href="/compare/amsec-security-scanner-vs-wordfence/">Wordfence Free</a>. For public file checks, see the <a href="/compare/amsec-security-scanner-vs-custonis/">Custonis comparison</a>. The <a href="/wordpress-security/">WordPress security checklist</a> covers steps you can take on any site.</p></div>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
